              "content": "Chapter 1 The Hahn–Banach Theorems. Introduction to the Theory of Conjugate Convex Functions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.332,
            0.894,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1.1 The Analytic Form of the Hahn–Banach Theorem: Extension of Linear Functionals"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.4,
            0.899,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let E be a vector space over R. We recall that a functional is a function defined on E, or on some subspace of E, with values in R. The main result of this section concerns the extension of a linear functional defined on a linear subspace of E by a linear functional defined on all of E."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.496,
            0.898,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 1.1 (Helly, Hahn–Banach analytic form).",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p : E \\to \\mathbb { R }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a function satisfying"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.103,
            0.55,
            0.137,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(1)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.103,
            0.549,
            0.747,
            0.59
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_15_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "p ( \\lambda x ) = \\lambda p ( x ) \\quad \\forall x \\in E \\quad a n d \\quad \\forall \\lambda > 0 ,\\tag{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.249,
            0.573,
            0.627,
            0.593
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_15_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "p ( x + y ) \\leq p ( x ) + p ( y ) \\quad \\forall x , y \\in E ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.606,
            0.898,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "G \\subset E"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a linear subspace and let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g : G \\to \\mathbb { R }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a linear functional such that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.641,
            0.615,
            0.66
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_15_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "g ( x ) \\leq p ( x ) \\quad \\forall x \\in G .\\tag{3}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.674,
            0.898,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Under these assumptions, there exists a linear functional f defined on all of E that extends g, i.e., "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g ( x ) = f ( x )   \\forall x \\in G ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and such that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.726,
            0.617,
            0.746
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_15_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "f ( x ) \\leq p ( x ) \\quad \\forall x \\in E .\\tag{4}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.76,
            0.899,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The proof of Theorem 1.1 depends on "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { Z o r n ^ { \\circ } s }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " lemma, which is a celebrated and very useful property of ordered sets. Before stating Zorn’s lemma we must clarify some notions. Let P be a set with a (partial) order relation ≤. We say that a subset "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "Q \\subset P"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is totally ordered if for any pair "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( a , b )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in Q either "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\leq b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "b \\leq a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (or both!). Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "Q \\subset P"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a subset of P; we say that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "c \\in P"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an upper bound for Q if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\leq c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for every "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\in Q"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We say that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "m \\in P"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a maximal element of P if there is no element"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_footnote",
          "index": 12,
